A thread's last word can go out of date when a series is revised,
applied, or withdrawn, or a decision changes elsewhere on the list.
Messages are immutable, so the thread keeps saying the old thing until
someone replies, and a reader who finds it acts on stale information.

The list researcher now reports threads made stale by newer mail or
trunk, and a pass sends each one short update citing what superseded
it. Updates are held to threads that are actually out of date, one per
thread, so grooming does not bury the list in noise. Threads whose
latest message is still in the inbox are left alone, since replying
would mark that message answered when it was not.
---
The new step goes after the reply as step 7, so Push and Verify become
8 and 9; nothing else refers to those numbers. It stays clear of the
lines touched by my open process-email patches, such as "[PATCH]
claude: Treat unintegrated mail as revisable drafts" [1], and merges
cleanly with each of them.

Pat's "need some way to close the loop on threads" reply [2] asked for
no automatic rule to close threads, and "Claude doesn't archive
email" [3] leaves archiving to the moderator. Updates here only ever
add a reply.

