-## 7. Push
+## 7. Update stale threads
+
+Groom the list as you go. If the list researcher found stale threads,
+or this pass's own work made one stale, for example by posting a
+revised series, send each one update. Messages on the list are
+immutable, so a new reply is the only way to correct one. Archiving
+is the moderator's job; an update never closes a thread.
+
+Send an update only when the thread is out of date, not merely
+related. Skip it if a later message in the thread, including your
+reply from step 6, already carries the new information, or if the
+change would not alter what a reader of the thread does next. One
+thread gets one update, however many points changed. Noise costs the
+reader more than a stale thread does. Leave a thread alone while its
+latest message is still in your inbox: replying would mark that
+message replied without answering it, and its own pass will carry
+the new information anyway.
+
+Build each update as in step 6, replying to the thread's latest
+message. Quote only the outdated lines, then say what is true now in
+a sentence or two and cite what superseded them, by full subject with
+the Message-ID in a footnote. Deliver and commit each on its own:
+
+    jj commit -m "mail: Update <subject>"
+
